Subject: wasix: wait on an interruptible host futex

A guest futex wait went through the async path: a logical waiter in the
futex table, a Tokio timer for the timeout, and the coroutine suspended
on a future for the host loop to poll. For a guest whose idle threads
wait on futexes with short timeouts, that machinery was most of the
host's remaining CPU.

Do the wait in the kernel instead. The runtime waits atomically on the
WASI thread's interrupt generation and the freeze-gate generation until
an absolute deadline, and on a guest futex word with its expected value
when the caller has one. A process-local futex in a linear memory whose
base cannot move is handed over that way. The kernel checks the values
and enqueues the waiter atomically, so no logical waiter is registered.
A signal or a termination bumps and wakes the thread's generation, and
closing the freeze gate bumps and wakes the gate's; either returns the
wait as interrupted, and the syscall falls through to the existing path
for the full signal and termination processing. A wake goes to direct
waiters first and to a logical waiter only when none woke.

futex_waitv arrived in Linux 5.16, so it is called through `syscall`
and probed once, on the first wait that asks for it; elsewhere, and on
a kernel that does not have it, every wait stays on the async path. So
does every wait in a process started with DISABLE_FUTEX_WAITV set,
which is logged at warning level, and every wait after one that could
not interpret its own result.

Shared-file futexes and dynamic memories keep the async path, since a
direct wait needs a word whose address cannot change. A coroutine frozen
in a direct wait retains it, so a resurrected child resumes the same
wait against its own gate generation. The wait lives behind wasix's
direct-waits feature, which the host and the CLI enable, and
nothing changes until suspension is enabled.

